'' A struct by value through a procedure pointer takes the ABI the procedure takes, and a
'' 16-byte result through one is stored. See the src file for the measurement.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --emit-ir tests/mir/src/procptr-byval-agg.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' A BY-VALUE AGGREGATE (a std module's, or the test's own): macos-arm64 refuses it until AAPCS64 lands.
'' EXPECT-EXIT[macos-arm64]: 1
'' EXPECT-OUT[macos-arm64]: error TKO0992: not implemented in this build: an aggregate passed or returned by value on macos-arm64

'' NO CALL PASSES A RAW AGGREGATE VALUE. That was every one through the pointer.
'' EXPECT-NOT: ([8 x i8] %t
'' EXPECT-NOT: ([16 x i8] %t
'' EXPECT-NOT: ([24 x i8] %t
'' EXPECT-NOT: ([32 x i8] %t

'' win64: 16 bytes and up go as a pointer to a copy, and the 16-byte result through a
'' hidden pointer. The define line of RET16 spells its own parameter '%p0'; '%s' is a
'' caller's slot, so this is the CALL.
'' EXPECT-OUT[win64]: (ptr sret([16 x i8]) align 8 %s

'' SysV: 24 and 32 bytes go in memory, and the 16-byte result comes back in two registers
'' through a callee that is a TEMP -- the pointer, not @RET16.
'' EXPECT-OUT[linux-x86_64]: (ptr byval([24 x i8]) align 8 %s
'' EXPECT-OUT[linux-x86_64]: .pair = call { i64, i64 } %t
